#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/08-regular-expressions.md:1024
msgid "A pattern that looks right can match too much or too little. Test it on good **and** bad examples, as the checks of the tasks do. [regex101.com](https://regex101.com) (choose the flavour *Python*) shows what each part of a pattern matches."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/08-regular-expressions.md:1029
msgid "Advanced: patterns that take forever"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/08-regular-expressions.md:1030
msgid "Patterns with nested quantifiers, like `(\\d+)+$`, can take a very long time on a long text that almost matches (\"catastrophic backtracking\"). Keep patterns simple, avoid `(...+)+`, and do not run patterns written by other people on big data without a test. Python 3.11 added *possessive* quantifiers (`*+`, `++`) and atomic groups `(?>...)` that prevent it - rarely needed in data work."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/08-regular-expressions.md:1038
msgid "Mask the account part of every IBAN in a text for a report: keep `BG` and the first 6 characters, replace the rest with `*`. Hint: a group for the part you keep, and `r\"\\1\" + \"*\" * 14` as the replacement."
msgstr ""

